    "recommendations": "Managing vegetation in the immediate vicinity of the structure should be a primary focus, as reducing the concentration of shrubs and woody fuels within and around the structure perimeter could meaningfully improve defensible space and reduce fuel continuity. Where vegetation is directly adjacent to or surrounding the structure, creating separation between plants and combustible surfaces — including walls, eaves, decks, and fences — may reduce structure exposure and support suppression operations. Thinning or spacing out shrub masses and vegetation clusters on the property could reduce the opportunity for fire to move continuously from the surrounding brushland interface toward the structure. Maintaining the access route by managing encroaching vegetation along the driveway or entry road would support firefighter access and improve evacuation conditions. Periodic ongoing management of mixed vegetation cover across the property, rather than a single clearing effort, would support long-term wildfire resilience and help sustain the defensible space over time."
